WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.741 --> 00:00:14.240
MIKE WILUSZ: Welcome
to Cloud OnAir,

00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:16.120
live webinars from Google Cloud.

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:18.320
We are hosting
webinars every Tuesday.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:19.415
My name is Mike Wilusz.

00:00:19.415 --> 00:00:21.790
And today, we will be talking
about how to enhance your G

00:00:21.790 --> 00:00:23.540
Suite efficiency with Chrome.

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:26.010
You can ask questions
any time on the platform.

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:28.431
We have Googlers on
standby to answer them.

00:00:28.431 --> 00:00:29.180
Let's get started.

00:00:32.234 --> 00:00:33.650
First, I want to
talk a little bit

00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:35.420
about the evolution
of enterprise

00:00:35.420 --> 00:00:38.277
and where end-user
computing has come to today.

00:00:38.277 --> 00:00:40.110
Today's movement we
call the cloud movement.

00:00:40.110 --> 00:00:42.401
But it actually starts way
back when we were originally

00:00:42.401 --> 00:00:44.124
talking about mainframes.

00:00:44.124 --> 00:00:46.040
In the 1990s, [INAUDIBLE]
became more powerful

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:48.050
with the rise of
the PC movement.

00:00:48.050 --> 00:00:49.680
So everyone started
to get computers

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:51.346
on their desks, which
were more powerful

00:00:51.346 --> 00:00:53.360
than the traditional
mainframe workstations they

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:54.770
were working with.

00:00:54.770 --> 00:00:57.080
This also brought
the rise of software.

00:00:57.080 --> 00:01:00.560
So end users began to install
packaged software directly

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:02.220
on their workstations.

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:05.420
This also led to an increase
in the need for software

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:07.320
from a security standpoint.

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:10.100
So this now required the likes
of things like anti-virus

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:13.520
and firewalls to be installed
directly on these workstations.

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:15.170
And then starting in
2000 with the rise

00:01:15.170 --> 00:01:18.920
of the internet movement, we saw
more and more computing power

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:21.590
being brought to the cloud
and was really the beginning

00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:23.210
of the cloud movement.

00:01:23.210 --> 00:01:25.480
As we transition
through the 2000s,

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:27.660
end users became
more and more mobile.

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:31.670
So this meant that users now had
more power that they had access

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:34.130
from from their actual
phones versus what

00:01:34.130 --> 00:01:36.260
they were traditionally
using on their desktops.

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:38.510
This was also the rise
of the social movement.

00:01:38.510 --> 00:01:42.620
So end users expected to
become more collaborative.

00:01:42.620 --> 00:01:44.270
They expected
inherently that as I

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:46.400
start to use tools to
work with my colleagues

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:49.340
that I would be able to
just inherently collaborate

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:52.230
without the need to
install desktop software.

00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:55.610
Continuing through the 2000s,
we actually saw a greater rise

00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:56.720
in applications.

00:01:56.720 --> 00:01:58.720
And this is where we
started to see people start

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:00.230
to bring tablets
to the enterprise,

00:02:00.230 --> 00:02:01.820
expecting that they
would constantly

00:02:01.820 --> 00:02:03.560
have access and
access to the content

00:02:03.560 --> 00:02:06.350
that they need with this
tremendous wealth of power

00:02:06.350 --> 00:02:08.240
of computing in the back end.

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:10.580
And now, here we are today
in the cloud movement.

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:12.980
And the interesting part
is that we haven't really

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:15.880
seen a transition from
the PC computing age.

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:19.580
We're still seeing a lot of
people with plain workstations

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:21.980
with operating systems on
top of those platforms.

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:24.510
They have to continue to
install security software.

00:02:24.510 --> 00:02:27.650
So as we've seen a transition of
data to the cloud and computing

00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:29.840
to the cloud, we actually
haven't fundamentally

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:35.092
changed how we have end users
access that compute resource.

00:02:35.092 --> 00:02:37.550
But think about what you're
already doing with your G Suite

00:02:37.550 --> 00:02:38.150
investment.

00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:39.830
You're already
adopting the cloud

00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:42.230
to give yourself a
competitive advantage.

00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:44.960
A great stat is 70% of
forward-thinking companies

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:47.780
on Forbes' Cloud 100 list
are active G Suite customers.

00:02:47.780 --> 00:02:50.749
Many of you are on
this webcast today.

00:02:50.749 --> 00:02:52.790
Think about some of the
value that that's driving

00:02:52.790 --> 00:02:54.650
for today for your end users.

00:02:54.650 --> 00:02:58.190
There's inherent intelligence
inside of these applications.

00:02:58.190 --> 00:03:00.800
Whether you're using Gmail
with suggested replies

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:03.320
or you're using our brand
new Hangouts chat product,

00:03:03.320 --> 00:03:06.110
you have access to a wealth
of artificial intelligence

00:03:06.110 --> 00:03:09.076
capabilities built directly
in to these applications

00:03:09.076 --> 00:03:11.450
without requiring that your
users are installing software

00:03:11.450 --> 00:03:13.370
directly onto workstations.

00:03:13.370 --> 00:03:15.470
There is a natural
culture of collaboration

00:03:15.470 --> 00:03:17.000
that comes with G Suite.

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:19.700
The users are naturally
looking to share content

00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:20.367
with each other.

00:03:20.367 --> 00:03:22.616
And they're expecting that
those connections will just

00:03:22.616 --> 00:03:24.514
happen inherently on
top of the platform.

00:03:24.514 --> 00:03:26.930
They're not thinking about the
devices that they're using.

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:28.888
They're just looking at
being able to reach out

00:03:28.888 --> 00:03:30.780
to their colleagues
to get answers.

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:32.294
Connected knowledge
is, I want to be

00:03:32.294 --> 00:03:34.460
able to search for the
content no matter where I am,

00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:35.720
when I want access to that.

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:38.920
Google Drive has really driven
a large innovation there,

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:40.420
where you have the
ability to access

00:03:40.420 --> 00:03:42.800
this content on your mobile
device or on your desktop

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:45.137
and, again, bringing
the capabilities

00:03:45.137 --> 00:03:46.970
of artificial intelligence
to do things like

00:03:46.970 --> 00:03:49.430
suggest documents for you
to work on the first time

00:03:49.430 --> 00:03:51.320
that you access your Drive.

00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:53.945
And then simple and secure
manageability-- as you know,

00:03:53.945 --> 00:03:55.640
the entire control
panel for G Suite

00:03:55.640 --> 00:03:58.010
is managed directly
within the Google Cloud.

00:03:58.010 --> 00:04:00.800
And this allows you to
not have to be concerned

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:02.060
about security capabilities.

00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:03.800
It's inherent to the platform.

00:04:03.800 --> 00:04:06.350
And we allow you to have a
level of controls from sharing

00:04:06.350 --> 00:04:07.730
everything publicly
that you want

00:04:07.730 --> 00:04:10.970
your users to be able to
share all the way to very

00:04:10.970 --> 00:04:14.840
fine-tuned data controls
to allow you to ensure

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:16.339
that your users are
not sharing data

00:04:16.339 --> 00:04:18.470
outside the bounds
of your organization.

00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:21.170
So you're already getting a
lot of the advantages of cloud

00:04:21.170 --> 00:04:22.756
by already being on G Suite.

00:04:22.756 --> 00:04:25.130
But again, traditionally,
we're seeing a lot of customers

00:04:25.130 --> 00:04:28.010
are continuing to leverage
existing workstations.

00:04:28.010 --> 00:04:29.810
And we think there's
an opportunity for us

00:04:29.810 --> 00:04:33.530
to innovate with our customers
to drive new types of business

00:04:33.530 --> 00:04:35.970
advantages.

00:04:35.970 --> 00:04:37.680
And we truly believe
that the cloud

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:40.710
is the spot where we're going
to help you be able to innovate

00:04:40.710 --> 00:04:42.430
from a security perspective.

00:04:42.430 --> 00:04:45.150
We're finding more and more
organizations are absolutely

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:48.610
adopting more and more
applications inside the cloud,

00:04:48.610 --> 00:04:51.450
whether it's things like G
Suite leveraging collaboration

00:04:51.450 --> 00:04:54.030
capabilities, to
ERP capabilities,

00:04:54.030 --> 00:04:56.970
to things like Salesforce from
a CRM perspective, all the way

00:04:56.970 --> 00:04:58.800
to popular HR providers.

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:00.870
We're seeing more and
more organizations

00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:04.830
adopt these platforms to become
a cloud-first organization

00:05:04.830 --> 00:05:07.200
to take advantage of
the security innovations

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:10.860
in the cloud to put their most
sensitive data, like CRM and HR

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:14.190
data, into these secure,
cloud-based tools.

00:05:14.190 --> 00:05:16.530
What we're finding is
because of this access

00:05:16.530 --> 00:05:17.989
to cloud-based
applications, users

00:05:17.989 --> 00:05:19.530
are expecting more
and more that they

00:05:19.530 --> 00:05:21.720
want to be able to
access these applications

00:05:21.720 --> 00:05:24.340
and use their capabilities
no matter where they are.

00:05:24.340 --> 00:05:26.798
So what that means is they want
to be able to-- whether I'm

00:05:26.798 --> 00:05:29.400
sitting on a Windows desktop
or I'm actively using my iPhone

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:31.800
or I'm sitting currently
on an Android tablet,

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:33.330
I want to have the
same capabilities

00:05:33.330 --> 00:05:34.716
and be highly mobile.

00:05:34.716 --> 00:05:36.090
And what we've
traditionally seen

00:05:36.090 --> 00:05:38.520
is many of these endpoints
aren't necessarily

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:39.720
catered to that experience.

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:42.600
But we're finding more and more
applications are allowing users

00:05:42.600 --> 00:05:44.250
to bring these
capabilities no matter

00:05:44.250 --> 00:05:46.004
which device they want to use.

00:05:46.004 --> 00:05:48.045
And unfortunately, the
number of security attacks

00:05:48.045 --> 00:05:51.210
is on the rise, and they're
becoming absolutely more

00:05:51.210 --> 00:05:52.540
and more advanced.

00:05:52.540 --> 00:05:55.350
We're seeing a great rise in
things like phishing attacks

00:05:55.350 --> 00:05:57.992
and targeted phishing attacks
called "spearphishing."

00:05:57.992 --> 00:05:59.700
We're also seeing a
rise sort of malware.

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:02.460
I just saw a recent
article that was discussing

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:06.030
the rise of ransomware in
a recent municipality where

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:08.040
they actually had to send
their employees home.

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:10.740
So already, hackers
know that there

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:14.190
is a target by leveraging the
existing endpoints out there.

00:06:14.190 --> 00:06:16.980
They know that there is a vector
to get into organizations.

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:19.140
And they're leveraging
that to then springboard

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:21.420
into other applications
that may be in the cloud.

00:06:21.420 --> 00:06:23.430
So we think this is a
really great opportunity

00:06:23.430 --> 00:06:25.555
to actually address the
endpoint security strategy.

00:06:28.180 --> 00:06:30.120
But what we have
found-- legacy endpoints

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:32.010
are really not built
to support security

00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:34.080
and certainly have
not transitioned well

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:37.060
into the modern computing age.

00:06:37.060 --> 00:06:40.119
You think about your phone when
you're using an application.

00:06:40.119 --> 00:06:42.660
When's the last time you thought
about things like a firewall

00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:44.910
on your phone or you've
thought about the capabilities

00:06:44.910 --> 00:06:46.950
of anti-virus updates?

00:06:46.950 --> 00:06:49.230
We traditionally, on
next-generation computing

00:06:49.230 --> 00:06:51.660
platforms, don't really
think about that stuff.

00:06:51.660 --> 00:06:55.500
But we're very used to, from an
end-user computing perspective,

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:57.450
putting an endpoint
out there and having

00:06:57.450 --> 00:07:00.870
to think about this entire
strategy every single time.

00:07:00.870 --> 00:07:03.760
And as organizations are making
the transition to Windows 10,

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:06.000
we have to consider
this all over again.

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:08.640
So whether it's partnering
with a virus vendor,

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:11.190
worrying about software updates
that we have to deal with,

00:07:11.190 --> 00:07:14.440
sometimes very last minute as
next generation attacks come

00:07:14.440 --> 00:07:16.500
out, patching that
operating system--

00:07:16.500 --> 00:07:19.150
the transition of Windows
7 to Windows 10 is a very,

00:07:19.150 --> 00:07:22.260
very large transition for
customers that a lot of them

00:07:22.260 --> 00:07:23.880
are taking their time with--

00:07:23.880 --> 00:07:26.400
upgrading hardware-- getting
a new piece of hardware

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:28.440
means you have to
create new images.

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:31.050
And then how do you backup
this data that is not currently

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:32.700
being based within the cloud?

00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:34.920
You have to figure out the
strategies and even things

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:36.520
like encryption as well.

00:07:36.520 --> 00:07:38.460
And we think this
is the opportunity

00:07:38.460 --> 00:07:40.440
to take a look at what
we offer with Chrome

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:44.530
OS and the capabilities we offer
to our enterprise customers.

00:07:44.530 --> 00:07:45.690
And let's think about it.

00:07:45.690 --> 00:07:48.090
Organizations are
driven to say, I

00:07:48.090 --> 00:07:50.850
want these two strategies
to be combined.

00:07:50.850 --> 00:07:53.610
I want my organization
to be more collaborative,

00:07:53.610 --> 00:07:56.670
to absolutely embrace
next-generation security using

00:07:56.670 --> 00:07:58.320
the capabilities of the cloud.

00:07:58.320 --> 00:08:01.080
But I don't want to be concerned
about endpoint security.

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:02.730
I'm not in the
business of needing

00:08:02.730 --> 00:08:04.379
to lock down my workstations.

00:08:04.379 --> 00:08:06.420
So organizations are coming
to Google and saying,

00:08:06.420 --> 00:08:07.970
there must be a
strategy around this,

00:08:07.970 --> 00:08:10.590
around giving our users
next-generation endpoints

00:08:10.590 --> 00:08:12.780
that they absolutely can
fall in love with and be

00:08:12.780 --> 00:08:15.270
able to leverage those
to securely access tools,

00:08:15.270 --> 00:08:17.940
whether they're on G Suite or
other cloud-based applications

00:08:17.940 --> 00:08:19.810
out there.

00:08:19.810 --> 00:08:21.840
And that's exactly
where Chrome Enterprise

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:23.301
comes into the picture.

00:08:23.301 --> 00:08:25.800
Customers often ask us what is
the difference between Chrome

00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:27.300
Enterprise and Chrome OS.

00:08:27.300 --> 00:08:29.280
And Chrome Enterprise
is the offering

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:33.299
that we have for both management
and support of Chrome OS

00:08:33.299 --> 00:08:34.214
devices.

00:08:34.214 --> 00:08:36.630
So the Chrome OS devices that
you can use as an enterprise

00:08:36.630 --> 00:08:37.919
are the same ones that
you could purchase

00:08:37.919 --> 00:08:39.659
from your existing
hardware vendors

00:08:39.659 --> 00:08:41.549
or go out to your
favorite electronics store

00:08:41.549 --> 00:08:42.600
and purchase those.

00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:44.850
And by applying the
Chrome Enterprise license,

00:08:44.850 --> 00:08:47.700
you now have the capabilities
to fully control and manage

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:51.330
that cloud-based endpoint and
pass down security policies

00:08:51.330 --> 00:08:53.280
and access a set of
applications that you

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:55.300
want to dictate to your user.

00:08:55.300 --> 00:08:57.420
And we've done many
advancements specifically

00:08:57.420 --> 00:08:59.820
in the Enterprise
for our customers

00:08:59.820 --> 00:09:02.460
to make sure they're
getting the capabilities

00:09:02.460 --> 00:09:04.440
that they need out
of the platform.

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:06.556
We've increased the amount
of productivity apps

00:09:06.556 --> 00:09:08.430
that are currently
available on the platform.

00:09:08.430 --> 00:09:10.800
Traditionally, it just
was web-based applications

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:12.360
and Chrome applications.

00:09:12.360 --> 00:09:15.180
We've now extended that
ecosystem to the Android Play

00:09:15.180 --> 00:09:17.430
Store as well, giving
us full capability

00:09:17.430 --> 00:09:21.090
to now install applications
directly on these endpoints

00:09:21.090 --> 00:09:23.280
and then also offer
updated applications

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:25.230
as they come to the Play Store.

00:09:25.230 --> 00:09:27.240
Built-in security
protection's directly in,

00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:29.220
which we'll talk a
little bit of later on.

00:09:29.220 --> 00:09:31.755
But something unique to
this platform versus what

00:09:31.755 --> 00:09:34.380
you may see from the competitors
or some of the other offerings

00:09:34.380 --> 00:09:37.140
out there is that we directly
update the operating systems

00:09:37.140 --> 00:09:38.170
right from Google.

00:09:38.170 --> 00:09:41.340
So that means whether you're
working with Dell, HP, Lenovo,

00:09:41.340 --> 00:09:44.490
whoever your favorite vendor
is, you can continue to leverage

00:09:44.490 --> 00:09:47.140
those endpoints that your users
absolutely fall in love with.

00:09:47.140 --> 00:09:50.660
But you're actually getting
direct OS updates from Google.

00:09:50.660 --> 00:09:54.180
So that means you don't have
to worry about those vendors

00:09:54.180 --> 00:09:55.890
having to certify
a patch before it's

00:09:55.890 --> 00:09:57.312
released to your endpoints.

00:09:57.312 --> 00:09:58.770
And this is more
and more important

00:09:58.770 --> 00:10:01.320
as we're seeing more and
more zero-day attacks being

00:10:01.320 --> 00:10:02.670
offered out there.

00:10:02.670 --> 00:10:04.470
The great part is
that it is tightly

00:10:04.470 --> 00:10:07.107
integrated to your
existing G Suite console--

00:10:07.107 --> 00:10:09.690
optionally tightly integrated,
which we'll talk about a little

00:10:09.690 --> 00:10:10.440
later on.

00:10:10.440 --> 00:10:11.898
What this means is
you can actually

00:10:11.898 --> 00:10:13.650
manage your entire
fleet of devices.

00:10:13.650 --> 00:10:15.840
So we're talking hundreds
if not tens of thousands

00:10:15.840 --> 00:10:18.997
of devices directly managed
from your G Suite console.

00:10:18.997 --> 00:10:21.330
All that requires is the
purchase of a Chrome Enterprise

00:10:21.330 --> 00:10:22.080
license.

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:23.790
And then you then
have the capabilities

00:10:23.790 --> 00:10:27.550
of managing all the policies
directly on that device.

00:10:27.550 --> 00:10:30.990
And automatic updates come with
support directly from Google

00:10:30.990 --> 00:10:31.900
as well.

00:10:31.900 --> 00:10:33.760
So that means if you
ever run into an issue,

00:10:33.760 --> 00:10:35.650
whether it's a hardware
issue or a software issue

00:10:35.650 --> 00:10:38.025
and you need to reach out to
Google for support on these,

00:10:38.025 --> 00:10:42.570
you get direct 24/7, 365 support
from Google Cloud support team

00:10:42.570 --> 00:10:47.290
to guide you through any
issues that you're having.

00:10:47.290 --> 00:10:50.580
And we think by combining
these two platforms together

00:10:50.580 --> 00:10:53.130
that you'll get truly
next-generation security

00:10:53.130 --> 00:10:54.600
capabilities.

00:10:54.600 --> 00:10:58.050
Piece of mind multi-layered
security means that you have

00:10:58.050 --> 00:11:00.281
the capabilities to--

00:11:00.281 --> 00:11:02.280
no matter what, whether
it's a piece of hardware

00:11:02.280 --> 00:11:03.810
or a piece of software--

00:11:03.810 --> 00:11:07.530
be able to make sure that the
policies are truly locking down

00:11:07.530 --> 00:11:09.396
the device and
making sure that you

00:11:09.396 --> 00:11:10.770
don't have to be
concerned about,

00:11:10.770 --> 00:11:12.269
oh, I have to update
the anti-virus.

00:11:12.269 --> 00:11:14.680
Oh, I have to update the
firewall on top of this.

00:11:14.680 --> 00:11:17.280
Hassle-free management--
built-in management directly

00:11:17.280 --> 00:11:20.070
from Google's cloud
for this platform.

00:11:20.070 --> 00:11:22.170
That means that from a
management perspective,

00:11:22.170 --> 00:11:24.614
you're going to the same
console that you're using today.

00:11:24.614 --> 00:11:27.030
And you're getting more value
from the existing management

00:11:27.030 --> 00:11:29.539
platform that you're
used to with less cost--

00:11:29.539 --> 00:11:31.830
and we'll talk about that a
little later on from an ROI

00:11:31.830 --> 00:11:33.990
perspective-- and a
consistent experience,

00:11:33.990 --> 00:11:35.740
no matter the type of
device you're using,

00:11:35.740 --> 00:11:38.010
whether it's a
Chrome OS Chromebook

00:11:38.010 --> 00:11:41.220
or you're using a Chromebox,
the same exact experience

00:11:41.220 --> 00:11:43.655
for your end users no
matter where they sit

00:11:43.655 --> 00:11:45.780
and which types of devices
they're currently using.

00:11:49.350 --> 00:11:51.560
So let's talk a little bit
about the protections that

00:11:51.560 --> 00:11:53.090
come inherent to the platform.

00:11:53.090 --> 00:11:56.210
You get automatic security
updates directly from Google.

00:11:56.210 --> 00:11:58.460
That means when that next
generation of vulnerability

00:11:58.460 --> 00:12:00.501
comes out, you'll come
directly to Google support

00:12:00.501 --> 00:12:01.220
for assistance.

00:12:01.220 --> 00:12:02.930
And those updates
will come directly

00:12:02.930 --> 00:12:04.850
from the Google Cloud
delivered directly down

00:12:04.850 --> 00:12:06.560
to your endpoints.

00:12:06.560 --> 00:12:10.430
Things that you're possibly
used to from the Chrome browser

00:12:10.430 --> 00:12:13.250
capabilities, but the
ability to actually isolate

00:12:13.250 --> 00:12:14.990
individual processes--
this is a lot

00:12:14.990 --> 00:12:18.590
of what you see on a lot
of modern mobile platforms

00:12:18.590 --> 00:12:19.560
as well.

00:12:19.560 --> 00:12:22.190
So this is essentially processes
cannot read data from each

00:12:22.190 --> 00:12:22.689
other.

00:12:22.689 --> 00:12:24.221
That allows them
to run isolated.

00:12:24.221 --> 00:12:25.720
So if there was
ever a vulnerability

00:12:25.720 --> 00:12:27.678
to a single application,
it wouldn't bleed over

00:12:27.678 --> 00:12:29.360
to the rest of the
operating system

00:12:29.360 --> 00:12:31.400
or to other applications
currently running

00:12:31.400 --> 00:12:32.666
on that device.

00:12:32.666 --> 00:12:34.040
And then other
browser technology

00:12:34.040 --> 00:12:37.370
that you're possibly used to,
things like Safe Browsing--

00:12:37.370 --> 00:12:40.700
so leveraging Google's cloud
to see if there's any nefarious

00:12:40.700 --> 00:12:43.280
websites that your users are
going to and preventing them

00:12:43.280 --> 00:12:46.140
from directly accessing
them on those devices--

00:12:46.140 --> 00:12:47.860
and then also
secure app access--

00:12:47.860 --> 00:12:50.750
so making sure the applications
that your users are accessing

00:12:50.750 --> 00:12:53.326
are delivered from a web
store or from the Play Store.

00:12:53.326 --> 00:12:54.950
So they've gone
through security audits

00:12:54.950 --> 00:12:56.420
before they're
actually delivered.

00:12:56.420 --> 00:12:59.690
Users cannot side load
executables onto these devices

00:12:59.690 --> 00:13:03.020
to be able to get around
security controls that you put

00:13:03.020 --> 00:13:03.960
on them.

00:13:03.960 --> 00:13:05.360
And a reduced TCO--

00:13:05.360 --> 00:13:08.685
we're really driving down
the price of these devices,

00:13:08.685 --> 00:13:10.810
finding a spot where we
think that enterprises will

00:13:10.810 --> 00:13:13.910
be able to use these at
scale at a price point that's

00:13:13.910 --> 00:13:17.570
not like many of the other
platforms that they're used to.

00:13:17.570 --> 00:13:19.130
Low end-user support costs--

00:13:19.130 --> 00:13:23.306
because these devices are very
easy to wipe, very shareable,

00:13:23.306 --> 00:13:24.680
we think it's a
great opportunity

00:13:24.680 --> 00:13:27.110
to also lower the support costs.

00:13:27.110 --> 00:13:30.800
Think of the time that
goes into OS management

00:13:30.800 --> 00:13:32.420
having to reimage a device.

00:13:32.420 --> 00:13:35.240
This is time that it costs you
from a support perspective.

00:13:35.240 --> 00:13:37.130
And we think by
leveraging this platform,

00:13:37.130 --> 00:13:39.350
you'll have these
capabilities built directly in

00:13:39.350 --> 00:13:40.890
and a future-proof solution.

00:13:40.890 --> 00:13:43.520
So as we continue to make
innovations on Chrome OS,

00:13:43.520 --> 00:13:46.310
as we continue to add new
capabilities like the Google

00:13:46.310 --> 00:13:48.837
Assistant, these will be
available to you as a customer.

00:13:48.837 --> 00:13:51.170
And you'll be able to leverage
these at your discretion,

00:13:51.170 --> 00:13:53.079
leveraging the control panel.

00:13:53.079 --> 00:13:54.620
So that means that
it doesn't require

00:13:54.620 --> 00:13:56.830
that you have to go out there,
buy a new device that supports

00:13:56.830 --> 00:13:58.100
that new operating system.

00:13:58.100 --> 00:13:59.420
You're getting direct
support from Google

00:13:59.420 --> 00:14:00.950
for these devices
for years to come.

00:14:03.570 --> 00:14:05.370
And a lot of the
controls and permissions

00:14:05.370 --> 00:14:07.650
that you're used to today
would also carry over

00:14:07.650 --> 00:14:09.190
to the Chrome OS devices.

00:14:09.190 --> 00:14:12.900
So you can really have great
filter policy and controls

00:14:12.900 --> 00:14:14.580
that you're putting
on these users.

00:14:14.580 --> 00:14:16.980
So this can be every
restriction from disabling users

00:14:16.980 --> 00:14:18.840
from going into
an incognito mode

00:14:18.840 --> 00:14:21.330
to actually controlling
USB port access.

00:14:21.330 --> 00:14:23.100
And these are as
simple as check boxes

00:14:23.100 --> 00:14:25.020
directly in the control
panel, leveraging

00:14:25.020 --> 00:14:27.103
a lot of the management
capability you're used to,

00:14:27.103 --> 00:14:28.494
like organizational units.

00:14:28.494 --> 00:14:29.910
You could set
sign-in restrictions

00:14:29.910 --> 00:14:32.201
to ensure that users are only
using the device that you

00:14:32.201 --> 00:14:33.580
want them to use.

00:14:33.580 --> 00:14:35.730
So this restricts
users from logging in,

00:14:35.730 --> 00:14:38.880
let's say, their personal Gmail
account to actually access

00:14:38.880 --> 00:14:39.540
the Chromebook.

00:14:39.540 --> 00:14:41.850
You can actually restrict
it to just your enterprise.

00:14:41.850 --> 00:14:43.500
And then whitelisting
and blacklisting

00:14:43.500 --> 00:14:45.990
of applications-- so ensuring
that your users are only

00:14:45.990 --> 00:14:48.390
installing the applications
from stores that you want

00:14:48.390 --> 00:14:50.080
them to be able to install.

00:14:50.080 --> 00:14:52.410
So we have full whitelist
and blacklist capabilities,

00:14:52.410 --> 00:14:55.230
all the way down to the Android
capabilities that we now

00:14:55.230 --> 00:14:56.940
offer on top of Chrome OS.

00:14:56.940 --> 00:15:00.060
This allows you to curate and
publish your own Google Play

00:15:00.060 --> 00:15:01.880
Store for your Chrome OS users.

00:15:01.880 --> 00:15:03.600
It allows them to
download applications.

00:15:03.600 --> 00:15:06.900
Or you can also force install
these applications on users.

00:15:06.900 --> 00:15:08.880
And we have a lot of
enterprise integrations.

00:15:08.880 --> 00:15:13.290
We just recently announced more
EMM partnerships in this space.

00:15:13.290 --> 00:15:15.180
So you can, if you
would like, continue

00:15:15.180 --> 00:15:16.740
to leverage the
control panel and add

00:15:16.740 --> 00:15:19.500
these end-user computing
capabilities on top of it.

00:15:19.500 --> 00:15:23.040
Or if you have an existing
end-user computing policy

00:15:23.040 --> 00:15:25.330
manager, you can continue
to use that as well.

00:15:25.330 --> 00:15:27.480
We've partnered
recently with VMware

00:15:27.480 --> 00:15:30.780
from their AirWatch product
along with some next-generation

00:15:30.780 --> 00:15:33.810
providers, like Cisco
Meraki, Maas360,

00:15:33.810 --> 00:15:35.220
and some other
providers as well.

00:15:35.220 --> 00:15:38.280
And if you're using existing
Active Directory integration

00:15:38.280 --> 00:15:41.190
today with your Google
control panel for G Suite,

00:15:41.190 --> 00:15:43.300
that carries directly
over to the devices.

00:15:43.300 --> 00:15:46.007
So whether you're using a
single sign-on provider,

00:15:46.007 --> 00:15:48.090
you can also leverage that
single sign-on provider

00:15:48.090 --> 00:15:50.677
to directly log into
these workstations

00:15:50.677 --> 00:15:52.260
and not have to worry
about setting up

00:15:52.260 --> 00:15:54.900
a whole different policy
to manage these devices.

00:15:58.100 --> 00:15:59.500
And really, I
talked a little bit

00:15:59.500 --> 00:16:02.230
about how we're helping
organizations drive down costs

00:16:02.230 --> 00:16:04.140
by going towards Chrome OS.

00:16:04.140 --> 00:16:06.100
There's the easy
part of things, which

00:16:06.100 --> 00:16:09.220
is these are generally
lower-cost devices.

00:16:09.220 --> 00:16:12.520
So compared to what we're
seeing in the marketplace,

00:16:12.520 --> 00:16:14.459
most devices that we're
seeing are actually

00:16:14.459 --> 00:16:16.000
under the cost of
what they're seeing

00:16:16.000 --> 00:16:19.930
from existing either
Windows or Mac workstations.

00:16:19.930 --> 00:16:22.180
The software licenses
are actually cheaper.

00:16:22.180 --> 00:16:24.640
So as I said, there's just
the Chrome Enterprise license.

00:16:24.640 --> 00:16:25.870
We're not requiring
that you go out

00:16:25.870 --> 00:16:27.340
and you license a
firewall vendor,

00:16:27.340 --> 00:16:30.190
you're licensing an anti-virus
vendor, an anti-malware,

00:16:30.190 --> 00:16:31.420
an encryption vendor.

00:16:31.420 --> 00:16:33.010
There's none of
those added costs.

00:16:33.010 --> 00:16:35.880
So you can actually drive down
your software licensing costs

00:16:35.880 --> 00:16:38.480
by leveraging this
platform as well.

00:16:38.480 --> 00:16:40.810
These are built to
be a mobile platform.

00:16:40.810 --> 00:16:43.640
Think of them very similarly
to your mobile devices.

00:16:43.640 --> 00:16:46.270
So we're very conscious
of power consumption

00:16:46.270 --> 00:16:49.000
and making sure we're keeping
that as low as possible.

00:16:49.000 --> 00:16:51.550
So we're actually seeing
reduced power costs as well.

00:16:51.550 --> 00:16:53.980
And from a support perspective,
I brought that up earlier.

00:16:53.980 --> 00:16:55.750
So think of the
time and money that

00:16:55.750 --> 00:16:57.490
goes into re-imaging
workstations,

00:16:57.490 --> 00:16:59.680
redeploying new workstations.

00:16:59.680 --> 00:17:02.740
Chrome OS devices are really
meant to be shareable.

00:17:02.740 --> 00:17:05.170
It comes from their
legacy in education

00:17:05.170 --> 00:17:08.109
where thousands of students
may share thousands of devices.

00:17:08.109 --> 00:17:10.150
And you want to be able
to swap those in between.

00:17:10.150 --> 00:17:12.339
And we think that that's
exactly what our enterprise

00:17:12.339 --> 00:17:13.630
customers are asking for--

00:17:13.630 --> 00:17:15.730
so the ability to quickly
set up a workstation,

00:17:15.730 --> 00:17:17.740
get a user up and
running immediately

00:17:17.740 --> 00:17:19.690
without the time
and maintenance that

00:17:19.690 --> 00:17:21.490
goes into re-imaging
a workstation

00:17:21.490 --> 00:17:24.020
and giving them something with
less capability than they're

00:17:24.020 --> 00:17:26.632
used to today, and then
administrative costs.

00:17:26.632 --> 00:17:28.840
This is a platform that's
built to be easy to manage.

00:17:28.840 --> 00:17:31.214
Hopefully, you see the same
capabilities with our G Suite

00:17:31.214 --> 00:17:32.420
platform today.

00:17:32.420 --> 00:17:34.690
So again, leveraging to
that cloud-based console--

00:17:34.690 --> 00:17:37.090
there's not a lot of agents
that you have to install.

00:17:37.090 --> 00:17:39.250
We're not asking for a lot of
different integration points

00:17:39.250 --> 00:17:39.750
here.

00:17:39.750 --> 00:17:41.890
And it's tightly coupled
with the investment

00:17:41.890 --> 00:17:45.100
that you've already made in
G Suite today-- so leveraging

00:17:45.100 --> 00:17:48.580
those management capabilities on
top of this end-user computing

00:17:48.580 --> 00:17:50.390
device as well.

00:17:50.390 --> 00:17:52.870
And then economic benefits--
so increased productivity.

00:17:52.870 --> 00:17:56.200
We are wildly focused on
making sure users are becoming

00:17:56.200 --> 00:17:58.900
as productive as possible
as quickly as possible

00:17:58.900 --> 00:18:00.040
on top of these devices.

00:18:00.040 --> 00:18:02.320
So if you've ever interacted
with a Chrome OS device,

00:18:02.320 --> 00:18:04.230
you know that they're
quick to boot.

00:18:04.230 --> 00:18:06.892
Some average between
six to eight seconds.

00:18:06.892 --> 00:18:08.350
That is the same
whether the device

00:18:08.350 --> 00:18:11.230
is booting for the first time,
booting after the users use

00:18:11.230 --> 00:18:14.290
the workstation for a while,
or booting after, let's say,

00:18:14.290 --> 00:18:16.010
an update that's just come down.

00:18:16.010 --> 00:18:17.710
So think about some
of the workstations

00:18:17.710 --> 00:18:18.640
that you use today.

00:18:18.640 --> 00:18:22.570
I just recently had to
update an existing device

00:18:22.570 --> 00:18:25.250
that I had that was running on
a different operating system.

00:18:25.250 --> 00:18:26.890
And that single
little update that

00:18:26.890 --> 00:18:29.950
had to be applied to my
workstation took 47 minutes.

00:18:29.950 --> 00:18:32.020
On Chrome OS, our
updates only takes

00:18:32.020 --> 00:18:33.650
six seconds for the end user.

00:18:33.650 --> 00:18:35.617
And it's completely
abstracted-- so again,

00:18:35.617 --> 00:18:37.450
trying to bring those
innovations that we've

00:18:37.450 --> 00:18:40.390
seen in the mobile space
to people on their laptops

00:18:40.390 --> 00:18:42.790
because this is what
users have come to expect.

00:18:42.790 --> 00:18:45.040
But they don't expect it out
of their computers today.

00:18:45.040 --> 00:18:47.470
And we think that we
can greatly change that.

00:18:47.470 --> 00:18:49.030
Reduced downtime--
we truly believe

00:18:49.030 --> 00:18:50.738
that these should be
a sharable platform,

00:18:50.738 --> 00:18:53.326
so being able to easily
share these amongst users.

00:18:53.326 --> 00:18:54.700
So if my workstation's
ever down,

00:18:54.700 --> 00:18:56.170
I could quickly
pick up a Chromebook

00:18:56.170 --> 00:18:57.970
on the other side of
the room and instantly

00:18:57.970 --> 00:18:59.907
be up and operational with it.

00:18:59.907 --> 00:19:01.240
And then reduced security risk--

00:19:01.240 --> 00:19:04.810
I've talked about this already,
but really baking in all the

00:19:04.810 --> 00:19:07.750
security capabilities that
you would come to expect out

00:19:07.750 --> 00:19:09.970
of a next-generation platform.

00:19:09.970 --> 00:19:12.460
Again, as you go out and you
purchase your mobile devices,

00:19:12.460 --> 00:19:14.830
you're not thinking about
how do I enable encryption

00:19:14.830 --> 00:19:17.710
on the device, how do I
go through and download

00:19:17.710 --> 00:19:19.390
a firewall, license
that firewall,

00:19:19.390 --> 00:19:21.580
and then license
that AV capability.

00:19:21.580 --> 00:19:23.290
We expect all of
that out of the box.

00:19:23.290 --> 00:19:25.770
And we think that
truly, end users

00:19:25.770 --> 00:19:27.940
should expect that
out of the box,

00:19:27.940 --> 00:19:30.230
as well as you as an
enterprise customer.

00:19:30.230 --> 00:19:32.716
So we bake all that
directly into our platform.

00:19:32.716 --> 00:19:34.090
There's no additional
management.

00:19:34.090 --> 00:19:36.673
And we're using next-generation
security capabilities like you

00:19:36.673 --> 00:19:39.610
see on a lot of mobile platforms
to reduce security risk on top

00:19:39.610 --> 00:19:43.002
of the devices along with giving
you controls and capabilities

00:19:43.002 --> 00:19:45.460
that if you want to prevent
things like data exfiltration--

00:19:45.460 --> 00:19:47.860
so preventing users from
logging into a device,

00:19:47.860 --> 00:19:50.470
prevent users from plugging
in USB thumb drives--

00:19:50.470 --> 00:19:54.040
it's as easy as a single click
to disable that via the policy

00:19:54.040 --> 00:19:57.169
manager in the cloud.

00:19:57.169 --> 00:19:58.960
And as I talked about,
we're really, really

00:19:58.960 --> 00:20:02.380
focused on making users
more and more productive,

00:20:02.380 --> 00:20:04.240
hit that power button
and be up and running

00:20:04.240 --> 00:20:06.280
and operational as
fast as possible

00:20:06.280 --> 00:20:10.220
is a core tenet to what we
do on the Chrome OS devices.

00:20:10.220 --> 00:20:13.060
So what we wanted to do was
bring additional capabilities.

00:20:13.060 --> 00:20:15.070
We've often heard, I
want to go with Chrome,

00:20:15.070 --> 00:20:17.050
but we're missing a
lot of applications.

00:20:17.050 --> 00:20:20.710
So we think by bringing the
Android Google Play Store over,

00:20:20.710 --> 00:20:22.210
that we're able to
bridge that gap.

00:20:22.210 --> 00:20:24.460
For the first time, we're
actually seeing capabilities

00:20:24.460 --> 00:20:28.060
like if a user needs to install
Excel on their workstation,

00:20:28.060 --> 00:20:30.490
you could actually
install Excel directly

00:20:30.490 --> 00:20:32.701
from the Play Store on
your Chrome OS device

00:20:32.701 --> 00:20:34.825
and, again, being able to
curate these applications

00:20:34.825 --> 00:20:36.640
for your users so
they don't have

00:20:36.640 --> 00:20:38.432
unfettered access
to the capabilities

00:20:38.432 --> 00:20:39.890
out there of the
Google Play Store.

00:20:39.890 --> 00:20:41.830
You can really lock them
into the applications

00:20:41.830 --> 00:20:44.830
that are core to your business.

00:20:44.830 --> 00:20:46.390
Often, our customers
believe that you

00:20:46.390 --> 00:20:48.430
have to be online to
leverage a Chrome OS

00:20:48.430 --> 00:20:49.637
device, which is not true.

00:20:49.637 --> 00:20:51.220
We actually have
offline capabilities.

00:20:51.220 --> 00:20:53.530
So whether you're using
next-generation progressive web

00:20:53.530 --> 00:20:56.087
apps or using Android
application capabilities,

00:20:56.087 --> 00:20:57.670
we have built-in
offline capabilities.

00:20:57.670 --> 00:21:00.070
So you could absolutely
continue to use these devices

00:21:00.070 --> 00:21:01.955
if your users are offline.

00:21:01.955 --> 00:21:04.330
And we think that if you're
leveraging the Chrome browser

00:21:04.330 --> 00:21:06.246
on desktop, which you're
hopefully using today

00:21:06.246 --> 00:21:09.124
to access G Suite, that we
want that to be a really

00:21:09.124 --> 00:21:10.540
seamless experience--
so if you're

00:21:10.540 --> 00:21:12.550
using existing Chrome
browser policies,

00:21:12.550 --> 00:21:14.980
applying those same
browser policies to users

00:21:14.980 --> 00:21:17.290
as they're working directly
on Chrome OS devices

00:21:17.290 --> 00:21:19.060
so it's a very fluid experience.

00:21:19.060 --> 00:21:20.770
And the expectations
of the user are

00:21:20.770 --> 00:21:24.160
met no matter which device that
they're currently working on.

00:21:24.160 --> 00:21:26.860
We also allow integrated
sync capabilities--

00:21:26.860 --> 00:21:29.200
so syncing applications
and user profiles,

00:21:29.200 --> 00:21:31.120
again, inherent to the platform.

00:21:31.120 --> 00:21:33.910
So a user, when they first
sign into a Chrome OS device,

00:21:33.910 --> 00:21:36.130
it actually looks like it's
almost re-imaging itself

00:21:36.130 --> 00:21:38.050
as it redownloads
all the applications,

00:21:38.050 --> 00:21:40.390
applies all the user
settings directly to it

00:21:40.390 --> 00:21:42.400
inherent to the platform
and the capabilities

00:21:42.400 --> 00:21:43.904
that we offer in the cloud.

00:21:43.904 --> 00:21:46.070
And, again, I talked about
the security capability--

00:21:46.070 --> 00:21:47.950
so making sure we're
isolating processes

00:21:47.950 --> 00:21:50.350
on top of these where it's
an Android application

00:21:50.350 --> 00:21:53.270
or you're actually installing
a progressive web app,

00:21:53.270 --> 00:21:54.820
making sure that
these applications,

00:21:54.820 --> 00:21:57.100
as they become more
and more complex,

00:21:57.100 --> 00:21:59.350
that these complexities
aren't causing security

00:21:59.350 --> 00:22:01.060
vulnerabilities on
top of our platform,

00:22:01.060 --> 00:22:05.670
something we're hyper-focused
in on on the Chrome team.

00:22:05.670 --> 00:22:07.730
And then what often comes
up is, I'm very used

00:22:07.730 --> 00:22:09.710
to Chrome OS in
education, which is

00:22:09.710 --> 00:22:13.430
really great if I want to go out
and purchase a $230 Chromebook.

00:22:13.430 --> 00:22:16.780
But it's a little different for
me as an enterprise customer.

00:22:16.780 --> 00:22:18.680
And there was actually
a large variety

00:22:18.680 --> 00:22:22.820
of enterprise-grade devices that
we offer for Chrome customers

00:22:22.820 --> 00:22:26.120
across a variety of
different manufacturers.

00:22:26.120 --> 00:22:29.060
For your front line worker where
you may have a more ruggedized

00:22:29.060 --> 00:22:31.430
use case-- this could be out
there with a field worker,

00:22:31.430 --> 00:22:33.500
possibly in a retail location--

00:22:33.500 --> 00:22:36.530
the Acer Chromebook 14
and Lenovo ThinkPad 13

00:22:36.530 --> 00:22:37.580
are great options.

00:22:37.580 --> 00:22:39.280
These devices are
meant to be shared.

00:22:39.280 --> 00:22:41.780
If they need to occasionally
be dropped for whatever reason,

00:22:41.780 --> 00:22:44.010
that's certainly
available as well.

00:22:44.010 --> 00:22:47.029
So you have the capability
to move these devices around

00:22:47.029 --> 00:22:49.070
and not be too concerned
about if the device were

00:22:49.070 --> 00:22:50.180
to suddenly break.

00:22:50.180 --> 00:22:52.790
And the nice part is,
inherently to Chrome OS,

00:22:52.790 --> 00:22:55.320
if the device is broken, you
could easily hand that person

00:22:55.320 --> 00:22:56.660
another device.

00:22:56.660 --> 00:22:59.740
And they'd be instantly up and
operational with their profile.

00:22:59.740 --> 00:23:03.420
And I need more of an experience
where it's, let's say,

00:23:03.420 --> 00:23:04.810
a more flagship device.

00:23:04.810 --> 00:23:06.470
It's got a better build quality.

00:23:06.470 --> 00:23:08.750
These may be fixed sitting
on people's desktops,

00:23:08.750 --> 00:23:11.750
or they may be assigned
directly to certain users.

00:23:11.750 --> 00:23:13.400
HP has built a
really great business

00:23:13.400 --> 00:23:15.980
Chromebook called the HP
Chromebook 13 with a really

00:23:15.980 --> 00:23:16.820
great display on it.

00:23:16.820 --> 00:23:19.520
That's been very popular
with our customers.

00:23:19.520 --> 00:23:21.770
Asus has also created
the C302, which

00:23:21.770 --> 00:23:24.290
is a great platform with
a great build quality.

00:23:24.290 --> 00:23:27.030
And the C302 is actually
fully convertible as well.

00:23:27.030 --> 00:23:29.210
So you can convert
it to tablet mode

00:23:29.210 --> 00:23:32.330
to access Android applications
and web apps directly

00:23:32.330 --> 00:23:35.030
on there as well just
by flipping it over.

00:23:35.030 --> 00:23:36.680
And really, for the
next generation,

00:23:36.680 --> 00:23:38.630
the person that
needs the best device

00:23:38.630 --> 00:23:41.210
within your organization, we
have a Chromebook for them

00:23:41.210 --> 00:23:42.090
as well.

00:23:42.090 --> 00:23:44.210
So Google recently
late last year

00:23:44.210 --> 00:23:45.880
introduced our Pixelbook line.

00:23:45.880 --> 00:23:48.770
The Pixelbook is a
device really built

00:23:48.770 --> 00:23:51.170
for next-generation
capabilities.

00:23:51.170 --> 00:23:54.020
So that includes a pen that
you can purchase optionally

00:23:54.020 --> 00:23:56.000
for the device as
well, built-in Google

00:23:56.000 --> 00:23:58.970
Assistant, and
next-generation processors

00:23:58.970 --> 00:24:02.480
all the way up to i7 processors
with 16 gigs of RAM--

00:24:02.480 --> 00:24:05.720
so really meant to be a
really powerful device.

00:24:05.720 --> 00:24:07.490
And Samsung has done
a great job of also

00:24:07.490 --> 00:24:10.610
building a pro-level device
called the Samsung Chromebook

00:24:10.610 --> 00:24:11.720
Pro.

00:24:11.720 --> 00:24:15.560
This also has the availability
to become a convertible.

00:24:15.560 --> 00:24:17.580
And it has a
built-in pen as well.

00:24:17.580 --> 00:24:19.380
This has been really
popular for customers.

00:24:19.380 --> 00:24:20.720
Samsung makes great devices.

00:24:20.720 --> 00:24:22.520
It has been a great
Chrome OS partner

00:24:22.520 --> 00:24:25.630
and building lots of different
varieties of form factors.

00:24:25.630 --> 00:24:27.380
And the Samsung Pro
has been very popular.

00:24:27.380 --> 00:24:30.020
And there's even a lower-cost
option with the Samsung

00:24:30.020 --> 00:24:30.950
Chromebook Plus.

00:24:33.960 --> 00:24:38.010
So we talked about the
economics, the integrations

00:24:38.010 --> 00:24:43.110
that go into leveraging Chrome
OS for your existing G Suite

00:24:43.110 --> 00:24:44.430
users.

00:24:44.430 --> 00:24:46.990
Overall, there is a lower
total cost of ownership,

00:24:46.990 --> 00:24:50.010
whether we're talking about
soft costs with how long

00:24:50.010 --> 00:24:51.450
it takes to spin
up a new device,

00:24:51.450 --> 00:24:54.000
how long it takes
to support a user.

00:24:54.000 --> 00:24:56.260
There's soft cost there
but also hard costs.

00:24:56.260 --> 00:24:59.700
These are generally
cheaper devices.

00:24:59.700 --> 00:25:02.034
The licensing on
it is much simpler

00:25:02.034 --> 00:25:04.200
because you're just purchasing
the Chrome Enterprise

00:25:04.200 --> 00:25:06.386
license versus some of
the other capabilities

00:25:06.386 --> 00:25:08.760
out there where you'd have to
license additional software

00:25:08.760 --> 00:25:11.670
vendors that exist
out there today.

00:25:11.670 --> 00:25:14.460
When you take a look at
actually integrating it directly

00:25:14.460 --> 00:25:16.900
within G Suite, as
you can see here,

00:25:16.900 --> 00:25:20.160
there is a much higher ROI
because you're directly

00:25:20.160 --> 00:25:22.410
guiding users towards
these applications.

00:25:22.410 --> 00:25:24.660
You're getting
the best of Google

00:25:24.660 --> 00:25:26.030
delivered to your end users.

00:25:26.030 --> 00:25:28.740
So quick endpoints to boot
up, get them instantly up

00:25:28.740 --> 00:25:31.380
and collaborative directly
within the platform

00:25:31.380 --> 00:25:34.560
in our highly-shareable device,
which is exactly what we try

00:25:34.560 --> 00:25:36.510
to offer with G Suite--
is the capabilities

00:25:36.510 --> 00:25:38.790
to be more social
with your documents,

00:25:38.790 --> 00:25:41.069
more social from an internet
perspective, offering

00:25:41.069 --> 00:25:43.360
those capabilities to be more
social and more shareable

00:25:43.360 --> 00:25:45.630
on top of Chrome OS as well.

00:25:45.630 --> 00:25:49.810
There is a hard time savings
that goes with this platform--

00:25:49.810 --> 00:25:53.910
so the ability to quickly
boot up, quickly shut down,

00:25:53.910 --> 00:25:56.460
that no matter what, I
always know that my device is

00:25:56.460 --> 00:25:57.620
going to be working for me.

00:25:57.620 --> 00:26:00.120
I'm not concerned about
charging it constantly.

00:26:00.120 --> 00:26:02.400
I have built-in
capabilities in here

00:26:02.400 --> 00:26:04.710
where I know that by
default, the device is

00:26:04.710 --> 00:26:06.930
going to boot up
quickly and be available

00:26:06.930 --> 00:26:08.960
to me as quick as possible.

00:26:08.960 --> 00:26:12.090
And increase in G
Suite adoption--

00:26:12.090 --> 00:26:15.660
these devices drive users to
really leverage the G Suite

00:26:15.660 --> 00:26:17.360
capabilities out there today.

00:26:17.360 --> 00:26:19.740
So that's things like
built-in offline capabilities,

00:26:19.740 --> 00:26:21.990
whether you're leveraging
things like Gmail or Google

00:26:21.990 --> 00:26:24.274
Drive inherently built
into the platform.

00:26:24.274 --> 00:26:26.190
And, again, it makes it
much easier for a user

00:26:26.190 --> 00:26:28.380
to flip open a
laptop and instantly

00:26:28.380 --> 00:26:31.410
get to the Google apps that
they need within G Suite

00:26:31.410 --> 00:26:34.200
without being concerned about
all the other details that

00:26:34.200 --> 00:26:39.230
go into them having to
use another workstation.

00:26:39.230 --> 00:26:42.210
I wanted to talk a few minutes
about a couple of customer

00:26:42.210 --> 00:26:44.070
references that
we have out there.

00:26:44.070 --> 00:26:46.260
These are existing
G Suite customers.

00:26:46.260 --> 00:26:48.750
Both of them have been on G
Suite for many, many years.

00:26:48.750 --> 00:26:51.000
But I'm going to quickly
read the quote from Whirlpool

00:26:51.000 --> 00:26:53.340
because I think it's a great
use case around leveraging

00:26:53.340 --> 00:26:56.140
next-generation devices with
an organization you're probably

00:26:56.140 --> 00:26:57.630
all familiar with
and probably have

00:26:57.630 --> 00:27:01.320
a little bit of their products
within your household.

00:27:01.320 --> 00:27:03.480
"At Whirlpool corporation,
we want technology

00:27:03.480 --> 00:27:06.330
to empower employees
to do great things.

00:27:06.330 --> 00:27:08.850
Chrome OS has helped
our organization do just

00:27:08.850 --> 00:27:13.230
that by enabling employees to be
their most productive whenever

00:27:13.230 --> 00:27:14.670
and wherever.

00:27:14.670 --> 00:27:18.030
Utilizing cloud-ready devices,
specifically Chromebooks and G

00:27:18.030 --> 00:27:21.930
Suite, has fostered a safer,
more secure, and faster user

00:27:21.930 --> 00:27:24.900
experience for both
customers and employees."

00:27:24.900 --> 00:27:27.450
As I talked about, this
is one of the core tenets

00:27:27.450 --> 00:27:29.580
that we've built
into Chrome devices--

00:27:29.580 --> 00:27:31.650
that I want to make
sure that my users are

00:27:31.650 --> 00:27:34.740
able to flip open a
workstation and instantly work

00:27:34.740 --> 00:27:36.290
when they need to be able to.

00:27:36.290 --> 00:27:38.730
And the device kind of
gets out of the way.

00:27:38.730 --> 00:27:40.920
This is what people
have come to expect

00:27:40.920 --> 00:27:42.280
with their mobile platforms.

00:27:42.280 --> 00:27:44.737
But they don't expect it
from the actual workstations

00:27:44.737 --> 00:27:46.570
that they're doing their
day-to-day work on.

00:27:46.570 --> 00:27:49.440
And by leveraging cloud-ready
devices that are directly

00:27:49.440 --> 00:27:51.450
integrated into the
G Suite experience,

00:27:51.450 --> 00:27:54.450
we think you can make your end
users even more happy being

00:27:54.450 --> 00:27:56.910
able to leverage more Google
technology to help them

00:27:56.910 --> 00:27:58.680
get their jobs back faster.

00:27:58.680 --> 00:28:00.240
And some really
interesting stats

00:28:00.240 --> 00:28:01.860
that came out of Whirlpool--

00:28:01.860 --> 00:28:03.840
that it only took
seven months for them

00:28:03.840 --> 00:28:05.370
to get payback on
their investment

00:28:05.370 --> 00:28:07.650
into the Chrome OS technology.

00:28:07.650 --> 00:28:11.910
It was an almost 300% return
on investment versus where

00:28:11.910 --> 00:28:13.320
they came from before.

00:28:13.320 --> 00:28:15.630
We talked about that lower TCO.

00:28:15.630 --> 00:28:18.480
But think about
the ability to have

00:28:18.480 --> 00:28:22.260
lower-cost devices in a variety
of form factors with simplified

00:28:22.260 --> 00:28:23.290
licensing.

00:28:23.290 --> 00:28:25.050
It's probably one
of the many reasons

00:28:25.050 --> 00:28:26.970
that you made
investments in G Suite

00:28:26.970 --> 00:28:29.580
is a simplified licensing
model, the turnkey

00:28:29.580 --> 00:28:30.630
ability of the platform.

00:28:30.630 --> 00:28:34.390
And that's exactly what we're
offering with Chrome OS today--

00:28:34.390 --> 00:28:36.510
and just generally
faster to deploy.

00:28:36.510 --> 00:28:39.390
I think customers
often look at Chrome OS

00:28:39.390 --> 00:28:42.060
and believe that it has
this legacy in education,

00:28:42.060 --> 00:28:45.600
so it's built to be just
an education-only platform.

00:28:45.600 --> 00:28:47.670
But most education
organizations--

00:28:47.670 --> 00:28:50.334
when they're dealing with
deploying end-user computers,

00:28:50.334 --> 00:28:52.250
they're not dealing with
deploying Chromebooks

00:28:52.250 --> 00:28:55.050
to 30 users or 50 users.

00:28:55.050 --> 00:28:57.450
These are often one
or two administrators

00:28:57.450 --> 00:28:59.610
having to deploy
sometimes thousands

00:28:59.610 --> 00:29:01.680
if not tens of
thousands of devices

00:29:01.680 --> 00:29:03.880
across their
end-user population.

00:29:03.880 --> 00:29:06.600
And as you can imagine,
especially younger students

00:29:06.600 --> 00:29:09.000
can be very demanding
of their devices.

00:29:09.000 --> 00:29:10.470
So if there's a
way to get in there

00:29:10.470 --> 00:29:13.320
and install applications they
want to be able to install,

00:29:13.320 --> 00:29:15.340
they're going to find
a way to do that.

00:29:15.340 --> 00:29:18.060
So that legacy from
education we're now seeing

00:29:18.060 --> 00:29:20.940
carry over into what
we're consistently

00:29:20.940 --> 00:29:23.370
hearing from our customers
in deploying Chrome OS

00:29:23.370 --> 00:29:27.090
and deploying next-generation
end-user computing technology.

00:29:27.090 --> 00:29:29.280
I want to be able to
deploy these faster.

00:29:29.280 --> 00:29:31.800
I want to be able to set a
policy and instantly deploy,

00:29:31.800 --> 00:29:34.555
whether it's thousands or
tens of thousands of devices.

00:29:34.555 --> 00:29:36.930
I want to be able to leverage
the management capabilities

00:29:36.930 --> 00:29:37.763
that I already have.

00:29:37.763 --> 00:29:39.630
I don't want to buy
another tool that's

00:29:39.630 --> 00:29:41.760
another server I have to
install or another cloud

00:29:41.760 --> 00:29:44.570
contract that I have to get
from another existing provider.

00:29:44.570 --> 00:29:47.790
I want this directly integrated
into the collaboration platform

00:29:47.790 --> 00:29:50.770
that I've already made
investments in here.

00:29:50.770 --> 00:29:52.680
So what we like to
say is Chrome's a very

00:29:52.680 --> 00:29:54.096
"set it and forget
it" technology.

00:29:54.096 --> 00:29:54.750
So you go in.

00:29:54.750 --> 00:29:57.120
You configure the
initial policy settings.

00:29:57.120 --> 00:29:58.590
And then from there,
it's very easy

00:29:58.590 --> 00:30:01.470
to deploy these
devices at mass scale.

00:30:01.470 --> 00:30:04.080
And once you configure
those policies,

00:30:04.080 --> 00:30:06.210
they carry across a
variety of form factors,

00:30:06.210 --> 00:30:09.180
whether it's a desktop form
factor or a laptop form factor.

00:30:09.180 --> 00:30:11.190
And now, just recently
in the education space,

00:30:11.190 --> 00:30:13.830
we announced a tablet
form factor with Acer.

00:30:13.830 --> 00:30:16.830
So being able to leverage
different form factors--

00:30:16.830 --> 00:30:18.540
and it doesn't make
a difference what

00:30:18.540 --> 00:30:19.706
form factor you want to use.

00:30:19.706 --> 00:30:22.290
It's the same exact
policies being applied.

00:30:22.290 --> 00:30:24.060
It just depends on
what capabilities

00:30:24.060 --> 00:30:27.610
that platform has which policies
are actually going to apply.

00:30:27.610 --> 00:30:29.894
But as next-generation
Chrome OS devices come out,

00:30:29.894 --> 00:30:31.560
you'll be able to
instantly adopt those.

00:30:31.560 --> 00:30:34.143
So when the Pixelbook came out,
if you were an existing Chrome

00:30:34.143 --> 00:30:36.367
OS customer, you would
just purchase a Pixelbook.

00:30:36.367 --> 00:30:37.950
You enroll it into
your domain as long

00:30:37.950 --> 00:30:39.325
as you have a
management license,

00:30:39.325 --> 00:30:40.950
and the device is fully managed.

00:30:40.950 --> 00:30:42.660
That's it-- no new
image to build,

00:30:42.660 --> 00:30:45.060
no additional capabilities
they have to go through and be

00:30:45.060 --> 00:30:45.726
concerned about.

00:30:45.726 --> 00:30:48.150
It's very easy to use,
next-generation devices

00:30:48.150 --> 00:30:50.480
built directly in.

00:30:50.480 --> 00:30:52.000
And another great
reference customer

00:30:52.000 --> 00:30:55.350
you may have heard of previously
is a company called Sanmina

00:30:55.350 --> 00:30:56.350
that does manufacturing.

00:30:56.350 --> 00:30:59.704
And they're been a really
great G Suite customer as well.

00:30:59.704 --> 00:31:01.870
And I just want to read you
a quick quote from them.

00:31:01.870 --> 00:31:04.420
"Chrome is empowering
everyone in the organization

00:31:04.420 --> 00:31:05.860
to be a cloud worker.

00:31:05.860 --> 00:31:07.460
With the help of
Chrome OS, we're

00:31:07.460 --> 00:31:09.760
a lot more efficient
and a lot more secure

00:31:09.760 --> 00:31:11.800
than we were before."

00:31:11.800 --> 00:31:14.350
Cloud worker is at the
core of what we're focused

00:31:14.350 --> 00:31:15.970
on here on the Chrome team.

00:31:15.970 --> 00:31:18.760
We want to deliver
next-generation computing

00:31:18.760 --> 00:31:19.810
capabilities.

00:31:19.810 --> 00:31:22.570
So we want to be able to adopt
the next great thing that

00:31:22.570 --> 00:31:24.310
comes out from an
application standpoint,

00:31:24.310 --> 00:31:26.620
whether that's progressive
web apps or mobile apps.

00:31:26.620 --> 00:31:29.069
So by tightly integrating and
listening to our customers

00:31:29.069 --> 00:31:30.610
and understanding
how do we do things

00:31:30.610 --> 00:31:33.130
like fill in application gaps--

00:31:33.130 --> 00:31:35.260
we've heard from customers
that sometimes, I need

00:31:35.260 --> 00:31:37.210
to run a legacy application.

00:31:37.210 --> 00:31:39.730
I need to deploy
an SAP application.

00:31:39.730 --> 00:31:41.080
Help me do that.

00:31:41.080 --> 00:31:44.530
So we have existing partnerships
out there with organizations

00:31:44.530 --> 00:31:48.580
like Citrix or VMware to be able
to deploy these applications

00:31:48.580 --> 00:31:51.760
virtually on the platform or,
again, offering the full Google

00:31:51.760 --> 00:31:54.460
Play ecosystem of
applications out there

00:31:54.460 --> 00:31:56.300
as well built directly
into the platform

00:31:56.300 --> 00:31:58.980
and fully managed via the
G Suite management console.

00:32:01.970 --> 00:32:04.990
And to get started from here,
if you have an existing G Suite

00:32:04.990 --> 00:32:07.480
account manager, feel free
to reach out to him or her

00:32:07.480 --> 00:32:09.640
to inquire about
these capabilities.

00:32:09.640 --> 00:32:11.920
As a G Suite customer,
you can instantly

00:32:11.920 --> 00:32:15.272
go out there and download
Chrome Enterprise licenses.

00:32:15.272 --> 00:32:17.730
So if you want to try this and
haven't tried it previously,

00:32:17.730 --> 00:32:20.332
it's as easy from your
management console to just try.

00:32:20.332 --> 00:32:21.790
If you go under
your Licensing tab,

00:32:21.790 --> 00:32:24.310
you'll see an option to start
a Chrome Enterprise trial

00:32:24.310 --> 00:32:24.827
license.

00:32:24.827 --> 00:32:26.410
We'd love to talk
to you and your team

00:32:26.410 --> 00:32:29.780
about next-generation
computing capabilities.

00:32:29.780 --> 00:32:32.110
So now, stay tuned for a
live question and answer.

00:32:32.110 --> 00:32:34.060
And we will be back
in less than a minute.

00:32:34.060 --> 00:32:34.560
Thanks.

00:34:04.877 --> 00:34:07.210
I'm now going to take us
through the question and answer

00:34:07.210 --> 00:34:09.469
portion of this and get to
all the questions they had.

00:34:09.469 --> 00:34:11.510
And there's some really
great ones here today.

00:34:11.510 --> 00:34:13.570
So I'm going to start
with question number one.

00:34:13.570 --> 00:34:16.090
"To get all of the support
mentioned, does the hardware

00:34:16.090 --> 00:34:17.679
have to be Google
hardware or can it

00:34:17.679 --> 00:34:20.560
be a Chromebook from any
device manufacturer?"

00:34:20.560 --> 00:34:22.639
So to get the policies
that I talked about,

00:34:22.639 --> 00:34:24.389
management through the
management console,

00:34:24.389 --> 00:34:27.820
or leveraging existing
EMM partners like VMware

00:34:27.820 --> 00:34:29.620
with their AirWatch
platform, you

00:34:29.620 --> 00:34:32.409
can use any Chrome OS device
that's out there today.

00:34:32.409 --> 00:34:34.752
So it does not have to
be Google manufactured.

00:34:34.752 --> 00:34:37.210
You can go and procure from
your favorite hardware vendor--

00:34:37.210 --> 00:34:41.300
a Lenovo, an HP, whatever your
favorite hardware is out there.

00:34:41.300 --> 00:34:44.080
You can take that and enroll
it to your management console

00:34:44.080 --> 00:34:46.719
and get the same level of
management capabilities

00:34:46.719 --> 00:34:47.287
on top of it.

00:34:47.287 --> 00:34:49.120
At the end of the day,
choosing a Chromebook

00:34:49.120 --> 00:34:51.040
comes down to your
favorite manufacturer

00:34:51.040 --> 00:34:53.469
and the type of hardware
specs that you're looking for.

00:34:53.469 --> 00:34:55.594
You make that choice and
you'll have the same level

00:34:55.594 --> 00:34:59.080
of capability no matter which
manufacturer you choose.

00:34:59.080 --> 00:35:02.890
"What kind of off-device log
aggregation is available?"

00:35:02.890 --> 00:35:04.540
So being cloud
connected gives us

00:35:04.540 --> 00:35:06.940
the ability to really
aggregate a lot

00:35:06.940 --> 00:35:09.187
of the data that end users--

00:35:09.187 --> 00:35:11.020
what their interactions
are with the device,

00:35:11.020 --> 00:35:13.294
what the current status
is of the device.

00:35:13.294 --> 00:35:15.460
So if you're coming from
the G Suite side of things,

00:35:15.460 --> 00:35:20.020
you're used to being able to
leverage our APIs to pull off

00:35:20.020 --> 00:35:22.152
different pieces of
G Suite information.

00:35:22.152 --> 00:35:23.860
And you can leverage
that same capability

00:35:23.860 --> 00:35:26.200
to actually use our
Chrome OS device inventory

00:35:26.200 --> 00:35:29.290
API that would allow you to
actually pull information

00:35:29.290 --> 00:35:31.390
directly from the
management console.

00:35:31.390 --> 00:35:34.120
And we're constantly looking at
ways to innovate in this space

00:35:34.120 --> 00:35:35.890
and bring additional
logging capabilities.

00:35:35.890 --> 00:35:38.260
But keep in mind, there
is a full asset management

00:35:38.260 --> 00:35:40.932
tool that is built directly
into this management console.

00:35:40.932 --> 00:35:42.640
So you can see the
status of your devices

00:35:42.640 --> 00:35:44.556
the last time they were
used, all the way down

00:35:44.556 --> 00:35:46.840
to the hard disk
current utilization

00:35:46.840 --> 00:35:50.140
down to the CPU temperature--
so really granular asset

00:35:50.140 --> 00:35:54.660
management capabilities built
directly into the device.

00:35:54.660 --> 00:35:56.370
"Is local storage
on a Chromebook

00:35:56.370 --> 00:35:58.650
encrypted, and how
is it encrypted?"

00:35:58.650 --> 00:36:01.290
So Chrome devices by
default are encrypted.

00:36:01.290 --> 00:36:03.930
Every single Chrome OS device
currently available out there,

00:36:03.930 --> 00:36:06.420
whether we're talking
about a Chromebox, list

00:36:06.420 --> 00:36:10.230
price in the mid $100s, to
the flagship Pixelbook device,

00:36:10.230 --> 00:36:12.360
every single one has to
be built to our reference.

00:36:12.360 --> 00:36:16.140
And that reference absolutely
includes encryption capability

00:36:16.140 --> 00:36:17.614
with a built-in TPM.

00:36:17.614 --> 00:36:19.530
That means we have
encryption all the way down

00:36:19.530 --> 00:36:21.900
to the operating
system, the hard drives,

00:36:21.900 --> 00:36:24.540
all the way up to the
individual user profiles.

00:36:24.540 --> 00:36:26.400
So as a new user starts
to use the device,

00:36:26.400 --> 00:36:28.479
they'll be encrypted
by default as well.

00:36:28.479 --> 00:36:31.020
And there's nothing else that
you have to additionally set up

00:36:31.020 --> 00:36:32.560
on top of the platform.

00:36:32.560 --> 00:36:33.870
It comes baked directly in.

00:36:33.870 --> 00:36:36.460
So there is no such thing
as an unencrypted Chrome OS

00:36:36.460 --> 00:36:39.330
device that would be
enrolled to your domain.

00:36:39.330 --> 00:36:42.060
Something else to keep in
mind is that all the policy

00:36:42.060 --> 00:36:45.960
capabilities are cached
locally on that device

00:36:45.960 --> 00:36:48.630
as well-- so, again, leveraging
that encryption that's

00:36:48.630 --> 00:36:50.160
built in inherently to it.

00:36:50.160 --> 00:36:52.140
And then you can also
control full capability--

00:36:52.140 --> 00:36:53.809
so making sure
users aren't taking,

00:36:53.809 --> 00:36:55.350
let's say, an
unencrypted thumb drive

00:36:55.350 --> 00:36:57.487
and plugging that into
the side of the device.

00:36:57.487 --> 00:36:59.070
You have the capability
of making sure

00:36:59.070 --> 00:37:03.180
that that's disabled from a
policy perspective as well.

00:37:03.180 --> 00:37:05.670
"With Chrome Enterprise, can
you download the Chrome OS

00:37:05.670 --> 00:37:07.080
version?"

00:37:07.080 --> 00:37:10.110
The answer is yes with a little
bit of a star at the end of it.

00:37:10.110 --> 00:37:11.670
So at any point,
if you ever need

00:37:11.670 --> 00:37:14.490
to roll back a device or
factory reset a device,

00:37:14.490 --> 00:37:16.770
we absolutely have
those capabilities.

00:37:16.770 --> 00:37:19.387
Again, this is meant to be a
very easy platform to scale.

00:37:19.387 --> 00:37:20.970
So if you're ever
at the point-- let's

00:37:20.970 --> 00:37:22.770
say you're testing
something in our latest

00:37:22.770 --> 00:37:24.870
beta channel or
developer channel

00:37:24.870 --> 00:37:27.720
and you want to roll back to
the previous stable version,

00:37:27.720 --> 00:37:29.370
it's as easy as
taking a thumb drive,

00:37:29.370 --> 00:37:31.290
plugging it directly
into a Chrome OS device,

00:37:31.290 --> 00:37:32.947
and running the
recovery utility.

00:37:32.947 --> 00:37:34.530
And the recovery
utility will actually

00:37:34.530 --> 00:37:36.691
flash a new version of
the OS to the thumb drive.

00:37:36.691 --> 00:37:38.940
And then it's as easy as
about a five-minute procedure

00:37:38.940 --> 00:37:41.840
on the device to actually roll
it back to a previous version.

00:37:41.840 --> 00:37:43.590
And we're continuing
to look in this space

00:37:43.590 --> 00:37:46.173
and see ways that we can better
improve that for our customers

00:37:46.173 --> 00:37:47.580
as well.

00:37:47.580 --> 00:37:50.550
"How effective are
Chromebooks offline?"

00:37:50.550 --> 00:37:51.540
It depends.

00:37:51.540 --> 00:37:53.550
It comes down to what
the applications are

00:37:53.550 --> 00:37:56.319
and what kind of data
that they're looking for.

00:37:56.319 --> 00:37:58.110
Like any other traditional
web application,

00:37:58.110 --> 00:38:00.000
if the user's
expecting to be online,

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:02.580
the offline capabilities
are going to be fractured.

00:38:02.580 --> 00:38:05.730
But we have offline capabilities
across G Suite built directly

00:38:05.730 --> 00:38:06.610
into the platform.

00:38:06.610 --> 00:38:09.390
So whether that's Gmail,
Calendar, or Google Drive,

00:38:09.390 --> 00:38:10.724
you have that built in.

00:38:10.724 --> 00:38:13.140
Also, keep in mind that if
you're leveraging things within

00:38:13.140 --> 00:38:16.020
the Android ecosystem-- so
you're putting Google Play

00:38:16.020 --> 00:38:18.600
applications directly
on these devices--

00:38:18.600 --> 00:38:21.780
a lot of those applications
leverage offline capability.

00:38:21.780 --> 00:38:23.604
It's a very popular
use case, especially

00:38:23.604 --> 00:38:25.020
in the personal
world where people

00:38:25.020 --> 00:38:27.306
want to use some
type of application

00:38:27.306 --> 00:38:28.680
out there to
download movies when

00:38:28.680 --> 00:38:30.240
they're offline on an airplane.

00:38:30.240 --> 00:38:33.990
So offline access is completely
supported on these devices.

00:38:33.990 --> 00:38:35.820
And it comes down
to the applications

00:38:35.820 --> 00:38:37.620
and how they're trying
to look for data.

00:38:37.620 --> 00:38:40.020
And certainly, we as
your technical team

00:38:40.020 --> 00:38:42.180
can go through your
list of applications

00:38:42.180 --> 00:38:43.687
and figure out
offline capabilities,

00:38:43.687 --> 00:38:46.020
making sure those could be
leveraged on top of Chrome OS

00:38:46.020 --> 00:38:46.920
as well.

00:38:46.920 --> 00:38:50.430
I will also note that there
are HTML5 offline capabilities

00:38:50.430 --> 00:38:52.510
built into the platform as well.

00:38:52.510 --> 00:38:55.230
So if there's existing
HTML5 offline applications

00:38:55.230 --> 00:38:57.600
that you are using,
those will continue

00:38:57.600 --> 00:38:59.070
to work on this platform.

00:38:59.070 --> 00:39:00.440
And those are baked in.

00:39:00.440 --> 00:39:03.330
So it's usually no user has to
go in and do any interaction

00:39:03.330 --> 00:39:04.514
or download an application.

00:39:04.514 --> 00:39:06.930
It's usually they just use the
web app as they're normally

00:39:06.930 --> 00:39:11.100
used to, and that data will
be available offline to them.

00:39:11.100 --> 00:39:13.005
"What are some of the
top security policies

00:39:13.005 --> 00:39:17.310
that IT utilizes the most
in the admin console?"

00:39:17.310 --> 00:39:18.840
So last I checked,
I believe we have

00:39:18.840 --> 00:39:22.262
over 250 different policies
inside the management console.

00:39:22.262 --> 00:39:23.970
So it's always a lot
to go through there.

00:39:23.970 --> 00:39:25.980
But I'll focus in on
some of the key use cases

00:39:25.980 --> 00:39:28.690
and what we've seen from
a policy perspective.

00:39:28.690 --> 00:39:30.510
So a very popular
policy we often see

00:39:30.510 --> 00:39:32.220
is just directly at
the sign on, which

00:39:32.220 --> 00:39:34.779
is if I'm leveraging single
sign-on capabilities,

00:39:34.779 --> 00:39:37.320
I want to be able to make sure
that that Chrome OS device has

00:39:37.320 --> 00:39:38.960
access to that SSO provider.

00:39:38.960 --> 00:39:41.370
So if you're using a third
party that's not Google,

00:39:41.370 --> 00:39:43.500
setting that policy
is very, very popular

00:39:43.500 --> 00:39:45.250
for Enterprise customers.

00:39:45.250 --> 00:39:46.860
Also, a very popular
device setting

00:39:46.860 --> 00:39:48.790
is something called
"ephemeral mode."

00:39:48.790 --> 00:39:51.360
What that means is users log in
and interact with the device.

00:39:51.360 --> 00:39:53.250
They're downloading
applications,

00:39:53.250 --> 00:39:56.116
let's say from the
curated Google Play Store,

00:39:56.116 --> 00:39:58.740
or they have other capabilities,
they're downloading documents.

00:39:58.740 --> 00:40:01.310
Ephemeral means when that
user leaves their session,

00:40:01.310 --> 00:40:03.550
it's automatically going
to wipe that session.

00:40:03.550 --> 00:40:05.430
So that's very, very
popular, especially

00:40:05.430 --> 00:40:07.380
in regulated industries.

00:40:07.380 --> 00:40:09.000
From there, the most
popular is really

00:40:09.000 --> 00:40:10.620
limiting the set
of applications--

00:40:10.620 --> 00:40:12.828
so making sure users aren't
going out and downloading

00:40:12.828 --> 00:40:14.422
applications they
shouldn't be using.

00:40:14.422 --> 00:40:16.380
So going through,
whitelisting and blacklisting

00:40:16.380 --> 00:40:18.750
certain applications,
extensions, and Google Play

00:40:18.750 --> 00:40:20.930
applications directly in
the management console

00:40:20.930 --> 00:40:22.744
is very, very popular as well.

00:40:22.744 --> 00:40:24.660
And then from there, a
lot of browser settings

00:40:24.660 --> 00:40:27.040
are very, very popular
for our customers.

00:40:27.040 --> 00:40:30.330
So that's things like
disabling incognito mode,

00:40:30.330 --> 00:40:32.820
possibly whitelisting and
blacklisting certain URLs

00:40:32.820 --> 00:40:35.010
to access or certain
URLs that have access

00:40:35.010 --> 00:40:37.350
to things like notifications
in the platform

00:40:37.350 --> 00:40:38.410
are very, very popular.

00:40:38.410 --> 00:40:40.746
We see a lot of
browser utilization,

00:40:40.746 --> 00:40:42.120
and that's a very
popular setting

00:40:42.120 --> 00:40:44.430
that users have available
to them in the management

00:40:44.430 --> 00:40:46.120
console as well.

00:40:46.120 --> 00:40:48.400
But thank you very much
for your questions.

00:40:48.400 --> 00:40:50.750
Please tune in next Tuesday
for the next Cloud OnAir.

00:40:50.750 --> 00:40:52.720
Take care.

